+Interaction and Editing Interfaces provide the user-facing mechanisms for defining, modifying, and exploring transfer functions (TFs) in volume rendering. They solve the problem of mapping high-dimensional data features to optical properties by offering intuitive, visual, or semantic controls that abstract the underlying mathematical classification. What sets this family apart from the classification methods themselves (e.g., [1D Transfer Function](11719-1d-transfer-function.md)) is that it focuses on the *input* and *interaction* paradigm—how the user specifies the classification rules—rather than the *evaluation* of those rules during rendering. These interfaces range from direct manipulation in spatial views to abstract parameter spaces like parallel coordinates or spreadsheet grids.
 
