 Image-based TF Design is a user-guided classification method where users draw scribbles on image slices to indicate Regions of Interest (ROIs), which are then classified using Random Forest on Implicit Neural Representation (INR) features to generate probabilistic volume rendering [7807-image-based-tf-design.md]. It solves the problem of manual transfer function tuning by automating the mapping from sparse user input to a full-volume classification [7807-image-based-tf-design.md]. Unlike its sibling [Sketch-based Transfer Function Editing](5943-sketch-based-transfer-function-editing.md), which typically maps 2D sketches directly to 1D transfer function curves, this method operates in the 3D voxel space using a machine learning classifier trained on spatial and feature context [7807-image-based-tf-design.md].
 
+### [Parallel Coordinates based Transfer Function Design](8511-parallel-coordinates-based-transfer-function-design.md)
+Parallel Coordinates based Transfer Function Design (PCbTF) is a method for designing multi-dimensional transfer functions by visualizing high-dimensional data parameters and spatial attributes as polylines in a parallel coordinates plot [8511-parallel-coordinates-based-transfer-function-design.md]. It solves the complexity of high-dimensional classification by allowing users to identify correlations and select parameters through polyline patterns, or by automatically reducing dimensionality using Local Linear Embedding (LLE) for k-means classification [8511-parallel-coordinates-based-transfer-function-design.md]. Unlike methods that operate solely in parameter space, PCbTF integrates spatial information as explicit parameters and leverages the structural patterns of polylines to facilitate dimension reduction and transfer function primitive design [8511-parallel-coordinates-based-transfer-function-design.md].
