 ## Background
-The concept of a "spreadsheet-like interface" for transfer function design was proposed by Jankun-Kelly and Ma [S1, unofficial]. Tory et al. proposed a parallel coordinates style interface for exploring rendering options and backtracking visualization history [S1, unofficial]. Tzeng and Ma proposed using ISODATA clustering on a small subset of all data voxels to classify the volume, displaying classified features in a cluster-space user interface to allow TF design without knowing the transfer function space [S1, unofficial]. Semantics by analogy for illustrative volume visualization was explored in [S2]. A Transfer Function Design Using A Knowledge Database based on Deep Image and Primitive Intensity Profile Features Retrieval was presented in [S3, unofficial]. Illustrative Visualization for Medicine and Science discussed multi-level interfaces in [S4, unofficial]. A volume exploration approach based on clustering and dimensional reduction to support the definition of multidimensional transfer functions was discussed in [S5, unofficial].
+The field of interaction and editing interfaces for volume rendering has evolved from direct manipulation of transfer function graphs to more abstract, data-driven, and semantic approaches. Early work focused on simplifying the specification of 1D transfer functions through direct manipulation [S1, unofficial]. As data complexity increased, interfaces began to incorporate multi-dimensional parameter spaces, such as parallel coordinates, to allow users to explore correlations between data features and visual properties [S1, unofficial]. More recent developments leverage machine learning and case-based reasoning to automate parts of the transfer function design process, allowing users to specify regions of interest in spatial views rather than abstract parameter spaces [S3, unofficial]. The concept of "illustrative visualization" has also influenced interface design, introducing levels of abstraction (motifs, materials) to cater to different user expertise levels [S4, unofficial]. Topology-based interfaces have been developed to link the structural properties of the data (contour trees) directly to the transfer function editing process [S5].
 
